&lt;div class=&quot;hs-featured-image-wrapper&quot;&gt; 
 &lt;a class=&quot;hs-featured-image-link&quot; href=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/the-quantum-leap-preparing-for-post-quantum-cryptography-featuring-verisec&quot; title=&quot;&quot;&gt; &lt;img alt=&quot;The Quantum Leap: Preparing for Post Quantum Cryptography Featuring Verisec&quot; class=&quot;hs-featured-image&quot; src=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/hubfs/2026-blog/verisec-pqc-blog.jpg&quot; style=&quot;width: auto !important; float: left; margin: 0 15px 15px 0;&quot; /&gt; &lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt; 
&lt;br /&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the PCI Security Standards Council’s blog series, The Quantum Leap: Preparing for Post Quantum Cryptography. This special, ongoing feature of our PCI Perspectives blog offers a resource for payment security industry stakeholders to share information about how they are taking steps to ensure their systems are crypto-agile into the future. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.wired.com/story/a-new-ai-tool-from-google-deepmind-formally-verified-the-signal-protocol/&quot;&gt;This&lt;/a&gt; seems like a good thing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Google Deepmind researchers used agentic AI to mathematically prove, or “formally verify,” a number of the Signal protocol’s key cryptographic guarantees­a landmark development for the widely used cryptography and AI itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Conducted as a sort of proof-of-concept project in just four weeks, the work is significant in two ways. It serves as further independent validation of the cryptography that protects massive quantities of the world&amp;#8217;s communications on platforms like the Signal app, Google Messages, and WhatsApp. And it demonstrates Google DeepMind&amp;#8217;s formal verification capabilities using a new AI framework called AlphaProof Code...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a current list of where and when I am scheduled to speak:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I’m speaking at the &lt;a href=&quot;https://luma.com/a3pk9zbg&quot;&gt;Catalyst Summit&lt;/a&gt; in Brampton, Ontario, Canada, on Wednesday, November 18, 2026.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I’m speaking at &lt;a href=&quot;https://queertech.org/en/events/qonference/qtq-2026&quot;&gt;QT Qonference 2026&lt;/a&gt; in Toronto, Ontario, Canada, on Thursday, November 19, 2026.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The list is maintained on &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.schneier.com/events/&quot;&gt;this page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Climate change is the &lt;a href=&quot;https://news.mongabay.com/2026/11/south-koreas-beloved-squid-may-vanish-from-its-waters-due-to-climate-change-heat-waves/&quot;&gt;issue&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As usual, you can also use this squid post to talk about the security stories in the news that I haven&amp;#8217;t covered.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2024/06/new-blog-moderation-policy.html&quot;&gt;Blog moderation policy.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This essay is adapted from &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eEBv0STiYhI&quot;&gt;a keynote speech&lt;/a&gt; delivered at the DEF CON 34 conference in Las Vegas on August 7, 2026. The essay originally appeared in &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.schneier.com/essays/archives/2026/11/ai-doesnt-have-to-go-rogue-to-break-the-world.html&quot;&gt;CyberScoop&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I want to talk about AI hacking. There are three aspects: people hacking AI systems, people using AI to hack other systems, and AIs hacking systems on their own, not even maliciously. The last two matter more than the first. The third is the weirdest and potentially the most destabilizing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I started writing about this in 2021 and in my 2022 book, &lt;em&gt;A Hacker&amp;#8217;s Mind&lt;/em&gt;. Back then I said it was mostly still science fiction, but not stupid science fiction. We&amp;#8217;re seeing it more and more, especially in the past year. The trends I&amp;#8217;m describing don&amp;#8217;t require a major breakthrough in AI. They&amp;#8217;re starting to happen now, and they&amp;#8217;ll happen more often as AIs get better at autonomous problem-solving. And it will involve far more than computer code...&lt;/p&gt;
